  Two consequences worth naming: [38;5;153mlogin[39m and [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m stop being globals (nothing currently reaches for them), and
  [1mopening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking will no longer work[0m — module scripts are blocked over [38;5;153mfile://[39m. Local development
  becomes [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m and visiting [38;5;153mhttp://localhost:8000[39m, which also happens to be what makes the [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m
  map entry meaningful.

  [1mOut of scope[0m

  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m stay as they are. They're CommonJS, make no API calls, and wiring them to an ES module
  would mean converting that tree for no current benefit.

  [1mVerification[0m

  There's no test infrastructure in the repo today, so as the design stands I'd verify by serving the page and
  confirming three things: the console shows the production endpoint resolving unchanged on the prod host, [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m
  resolves to the local API, and an unlisted hostname throws with the message above. That's manual, and it's the weakest
  part of this design — which leads to the last question.

  The repo has no linting, no formatter, no test runner, and no build. This is the cheapest moment to add any of them.
  [38;5;153mresolveSettings[39m is a pure function with an error path, so it's the rare bit of this codebase that's genuinely worth a
  test.
